fn merge_sorted(a: &[i32], b: &[i32]) -> Vec<i32> {
    let mut out = Vec::with_capacity(a.len() + b.len());
    let (mut i, mut j) = (0, 0);
    while i < a.len() && j < b.len() {
        if a[i] <= b[j] {
            out.push(a[i]);
            i += 1;
        } else {
            out.push(b[j]);
            j += 1;
        }
    }
    out.extend_from_slice(&a[i..]);
    out.extend_from_slice(&b[j..]);
    out
}

#[test]
fn handles_empty_and_equal_values() {
    assert_eq!(merge_sorted(&[], &[]), Vec::<i32>::new());
    assert_eq!(merge_sorted(&[], &[1, 2]), vec![1, 2]);
    assert_eq!(merge_sorted(&[2, 2], &[2, 3]), vec![2, 2, 2, 3]);
    assert_eq!(merge_sorted(&[-4, 1, 7], &[-2, 1, 8]), vec![-4, -2, 1, 1, 7, 8]);
}

The function reads each input element once and appends it once. For input lengths n and m, the time cost is O(n + m). The output requires O(n + m) space. The two indices use constant extra space. Choosing the left value when both values are equal preserves their order within each input. The slice parameters let the function borrow the inputs without changing them. The caller must supply inputs that are already sorted.

